{"title":"Working with Sections","markdown":{"headingText":"Working with Sections","headingAttr":{"id":"sec-sections","classes":[],"keyvalue":[]},"containsRefs":false,"markdown":"\nSection headings create document structure automatically.\n\n```markdown\n# Introduction\n\n## Background\n\n## Methods\n\n# Results\n\n# Conclusions\n```\n\nQuarto can automatically number sections using:\n\n```yaml\nformat:\n  html:\n    number-sections: true\n```\n\n## How sections are displayed\n\nEach separate .qmd file will appear in the left hand navigation menu as a (numbered) chapter. \n\nWithin each .qmd, each heading level will appear in the table of contents (right hand side)\n\n- Heading level 1 `#` will increment the number (e.g. 1,2,3 etc)\n- Heading level 2 `##` will increment the subpoints (e.g. 1.1, 1.2, 1.3 etc)\n- Heading level 3 `###` will increment the sub-subpoint (e.g. 1.1.1, 1.1.2, 1.1.3 etc)\n\n## Numbering sections\n\nSections are numbered by default (`# Section heading`). You can create a unnumbered heading with `{.unnumbered}` (e.g. )`# Unnumbered section heading {.unnumbered}`)\n\n### Global defaults\nNumbered sections are the default and can be controlled in the yaml\n\n```markdown\nformat:\n  html:\n    number-sections: true\n    toc: true\n    toc-depth: 3\n    toc-location: right\n  pdf:\n    number-sections: true\n```\n\n## Labelling sections\n\nSections can be assigned identifiers.\n\n```markdown\n## Newton's Laws {#sec-newton}\n```\n\n::: {.callout-important}\n\nYou should use `-` rather than `_` in section labels as underscores can confuse Quarto!\n\n:::\n\n\n## Referencing sections\n\nYou can then reference the section elsewhere:\n\n```markdown\nSee @sec-newton for an introduction to Newton's Laws.\n```\n\nWhen rendered, Quarto automatically inserts the appropriate section number.\n","srcMarkdownNoYaml":""},"formats":{"html":{"identifier":{"display-name":"HTML","target-format":"html","base-format":"html"},"execute":{"fig-width":7,"fig-height":5,"fig-